Among those doors, the door receives its name because of the action, and the action receives its name because of the door. Just as the places of arising for consciousness etc., receive the names door of consciousness (viññāṇadvāra), door of contact (phassadvāra), door of non-restraint (asaṃvaradvāra), and door of restraint (saṃvaradvāra), so too the place of arising of bodily action (bodily intimation/kāyaviññatti) receives the name door of bodily action (kāyakammadvāra). The same method applies to the doors of verbal and mental action. Furthermore, just as the deities residing in various trees receive names based on those trees, such as the silk-cotton tree deity (simbalidevatā), the flame-of-the-forest tree deity (palāsadevatā), the neem tree deity (pucimandadevatā), and the phandana tree deity, so too the action performed through the body-door receives its name based on the door as 'bodily action' (kāyakamma). The same method applies to verbal action and mental action. In receiving such names, although the body is one thing and the action is another, because it is performed by the body, that action is called bodily action. Therefore, the teachers of the Commentaries (Aṭṭhakathācariya) said this:

If an action is performed by the body, it is called bodily action;
The body and bodily action are established as distinct from each other.
If a work is performed with a needle, it is called needle-work;
The needle and the needle-work are established as distinct from each other.
If a work is performed with an adze, it is called adze-work;
The adze and the adze-work are established as distinct from each other.
If a work is performed by a man, it is called man's work;
The man and the man's work are established as distinct from each other.

## Even so.

If an action is performed by the body, it is called bodily action;
The body and bodily action are established as distinct from each other.

If this is so, then the definition of the door is not appropriate, nor is the definition of the action appropriate. Why? Because according to the statement "actions range in the doors," verbal action also arises in bodily intimation (kāyaviññatti); therefore, it is not appropriate to define that bodily intimation as the door of bodily action. Bodily action also arises in verbal intimation (vacīviññatti); therefore, it is not appropriate to define that action as bodily action (so it might be objected). It is not that it is inappropriate; it is appropriate. Why? It is appropriate both because of the prevalence of its occurrence and because that action occurs frequently in that door. Only bodily action mostly arises in bodily intimation; the others (verbal and mental actions) do not. Therefore, because bodily action mostly arises there, the status of that bodily intimation as the door of bodily action is established. Just as a village of Brahmins, a mango grove, or a nagavanna grove etc., are designated as 'Brahmin village' etc., so the definition of the door is appropriate. Bodily action also occurs mostly in the body-door; only a little...
